   145	## §2 The AST tuple grammar
   146	
   147	### 2.1 Form
   148	
   149	The map AST is the code's own representation — the **semantic layer**. A
   150	node is a map of its tag and its named fields:
   151	
   152	```clojure
   153	{:type :lambda,
   154	 :params [x],
   155	 :body {:type :application,
   156	        :operator {:type :variable, :name +},
   157	        :operands [{:type :variable, :name x} {:type :literal, :value 1}],
   158	        :tail? true}}
   159	```
   160	
   161	This is exactly what the walker's `case` dispatches on today: every arm
   162	of the cold case (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:359-469`) and the
   163	hot case (`ast_walker.cljc:600-626`) keys on the node's `:type` and
   164	reads a fixed set of named fields. The walker stays on this form, and so
   165	does every test that constructs map ASTs; nothing in this design sweeps
   166	them. But the map AST is never hashed directly, never stored, never
   167	shipped. It exists on both sides of storage — frontend-side, where
   168	frontends emit it, and machine-side, where the loader reconstructs it
   169	(§7.1) — and it is ephemeral and per-consumer, like an image: each
   170	consumer's map is its own value, and none of them is the canonical one.
   171	
   172	The canonical artifact is the **flat per-node row projection** of the map
   173	AST, `project : map-ast → rows`, one row per node:
   174	
   175	```clojure
   176	[A :lambda [x] B]
   177	[B :application C [D E] true]
   178	[C :variable +]
   179	[D :variable x]
   180	[E :literal 1]          ; A..E are :segment/sha256-… row ids
   181	```
   182	
   183	A row's **body** is `[tag & slots]`: the slots are the node's field
   184	values in the §2.3 order — a child slot holds the child row's id, a
   185	`nodes` slot holds the ordered vector of the children's ids, and every
   186	other slot holds its data unchanged. The row's id is
   187	`(dao.jing/segment-key body)` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:217-225`), and the
   188	stored row is the id prepended to its own body, `[id tag & slots]`. The
   189	id is therefore merkle: a parent's id commits to its children's ids
   190	inline, and a tree's address is its root row's id (§4.1). These rows are
   191	what `dao.jing` stores, what streams carry, what addresses name, and
   192	what `q` queries. **There is no nested tuple tree anywhere in this
   193	design**: the rows are not interleaved into a tree-shaped value, and no
   194	sentence below may be read as one; the tree is the map AST the rows
   195	reconstruct to. Identical subtrees project to the identical row, so a
   196	stored set holds one row per distinct subtree and is the tree's DAG
   197	(§4.4). Whether `dao.jing` stores rows as individual values (git-style,
   198	shared subtrees shared) or packs them per tree is an **Open Question**,
   199	not just an implementation note, because the choice determines the loader's interface (N fetches vs 1 fetch) and the validator's reachability rules. If packed, the pack requires its own address distinct from the root id.
   200	
   201	The projection is total and lossless by the round-trip law (§7.2): for
   202	every corpus program, `map → rows → map` is the identity on the
   203	*canonical* map AST (post-strip and post-saturate, with frontend `:yang/*` keys and stray `tail?` marks gone), and `rows → map → rows` is the identity on the rows.
   204	The §2.3 table is the dictionary of both directions — a tag's slot list,
   205	in order, is the map's key set, so one table fixes the row's positions
   206	and the map's fields together.
   207	
   208	**Runtime `assoc` into a map node is legal.** The node a walker holds
   209	under `:frame` is the machine's ephemeral image of the code, not a
   210	canonical value, and nothing runtime-`assoc`ed is ever projected back to
   211	rows: the cold `:eval-operator` arm `assoc`s `:operator-evaluated?` and
   212	`:fn` into it (`ast_walker.cljc:213-217`), the cold `:eval-operand` arm
   213	`assoc`s `:evaluated` (`ast_walker.cljc:226-237`), and the hot arms do
   214	the same (`ast_walker.cljc:532-541`, `:576-580`); all of it stays inside
   215	the machine. What the boundary requires is only that a projection's
   216	input be a well-formed map AST (§2.4, §2.5); the reverse direction never
   217	re-derives what evaluation wrote (§2.6).
   218	
   219	### 2.2 Slot kinds
   220	
   221	Every slot in the table has one kind. The kind is fixed by the tag and the
   222	position; it is never inferred from the value. A literal whose value happens
   223	to look like a node is a value, which is the rule `linearize/ast-children`
   224	already states (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:230-242`).
